--- Rosetta Code: Stair-climbing puzzle
--- https://rosettacode.org/wiki/Stair-climbing_puzzle
--- step is the task's API: it takes nothing, a seeded draw decides whether the attempt
--- climbs or falls, the attempt is told as its side effect, and the answer is whether
--- it climbed. step_up is the task's recursion, with no variable and no number: after
--- a fall, climb twice; step_up_loop is its loop form, climbing once after each fall
--- until a step holds. (A program keeps no state between calls but what it holds, so
--- the robot's height is not kept: each attempt says what it did.)
--- How deep the recursion goes: each call is made by a fall in the call below it, so
--- a climb is never deeper than one call more than its falls, and the loop form is
--- exactly one call deeper than the robot is below where the climb began. A fair robot
--- would end every climb but could fall any distance on the way (the attempts a climb
--- takes have no finite mean), so this one climbs two times in three: it ever falls k
--- steps below its start with chance at most (1/2)^k, and the depth stays a few calls,
--- far inside Crow's limit of 10,000 nested calls. The seed makes every run the same.

use math

fn step[]
    if rand[3] gt 0
        log['    step: climbed']
        return true
    log['    step: fell']
    return false

fn step_up[]
    if not step[]
        step_up[]
        step_up[]

fn step_up_loop[]
    while not step[]
        step_up_loop[]

seed[7]
log['step_up, the recursive form (after a fall, climb twice):']
for n in range[1, 6]
    log['  climb `n`:']
    step_up[]
log['']
log['step_up, the loop form (climb once after each fall, until a step holds):']
for n in range[1, 6]
    log['  climb `n`:']
    step_up_loop[]
